---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AcademyMap from './AcademyMap.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Academy — Sekiya"
  description="A cabinet of visual technical notebooks: open a mechanism and take it apart."
  objectCount={2}
>
  <header class="academy-hero">
    <div class="shell academy-hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Sekiya Academy / curiosity has no prerequisites</p>
      <h1>Open a mechanism.<br /><em>See what moves.</em></h1>
      <p>
        A small, growing set of rooms, each one a mechanism taken apart until it's obvious.
        No badges, no obligation to finish anything — but a real order, if you want one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <AcademyMap />
  </header>

  <section class="academy-room shell" aria-labelledby="open-rooms">
    <div class="academy-room__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Rooms currently open</p>
      <h2 id="open-rooms">Two ways into the machine.</h2>
      <p>Recovered from the GPU engine’s real implementation notes, then rebuilt as short, visual explanations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="notebook-grid">
      <a class="notebook notebook--gpu" href="/academy/gpu-command-journey/">
        <div class="notebook__visual" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="notebook__chip">
            {Array.from({ length: 35 }, (_, i) => <i style={`--i:${i}`}></i>)}
          </div>
          <span class="notebook__beam"></span>
        </div>
        <span class="notebook__meta">GPU / Trace / Interactive</span>
        <h3>One command’s journey through the GPU</h3>
        <p>Buffers, binding, encoding, dispatch, and why the CPU cannot simply reach in.</p>
        <span class="notebook__enter">Enter room <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></span>
      </a>

      <a class="notebook notebook--ping" href="/academy/ping-pong-buffers/">
        <div class="notebook__visual" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="notebook__buffer">A</div>
          <span></span>
          <div class="notebook__buffer">B</div>
        </div>
        <span class="notebook__meta">Simulation / Visual / From scratch</span>
        <h3>Why simulations need two futures</h3>
        <p>Read here, write there, swap the names. One tiny pattern prevents a world from tearing itself apart.</p>
        <span class="notebook__enter">Enter room <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="academy-cabinet">
    <div class="shell academy-cabinet__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Recovered shelf / GPU simulation engine</p>
        <h2>Thirteen useful things survived.</h2>
        <p class="academy-cabinet__lede">
          Practical notes written while building a GPU-first simulation engine. The old
          identity stays in history; the mechanisms are still worth opening. Grouped the way
          a course would group them, not the order they were written in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="academy-syllabus">
        <div class="academy-syllabus__group">
          <p class="academy-syllabus__label">01 · Execution</p>
          <ol class="academy-index">
            <li class="is-open"><span>01</span><strong>WebGPU compute mental model</strong><em>open room</em><p>Why the GPU is thousands of tiny copies of one instruction, not one fast CPU.</p></li>
            <li><span>02</span><strong>Dispatch and thread organisation</strong><em>in room 01</em><p>Choosing workgroup size and covering the tail when the count doesn't divide evenly.</p></li>
            <li><span>03</span><strong>Buffer layouts and alignment</strong><em>recovered</em><p>Why WGSL structs need explicit padding to match what the CPU wrote.</p></li>
            <li><span>04</span><strong>Atomics and race conditions</strong><em>recovered</em><p>What actually happens when two invocations write the same address at once.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="academy-syllabus__group">
          <p class="academy-syllabus__label">02 · State &amp; correctness</p>
          <ol class="academy-index">
            <li class="is-open"><span>05</span><strong>Ping-pong buffers</strong><em>open room</em><p>Reading and writing the same world at once quietly changes the rules — the two-buffer fix.</p></li>
            <li><span>06</span><strong>Determinism in simulation</strong><em>recovered</em><p>What has to be fixed — seed, order, precision — before "run it again" means anything.</p></li>
            <li><span>07</span><strong>Parity tests and golden fixtures</strong><em>recovered</em><p>Proving a rewrite behaves like the original, not just that it compiles.</p></li>
            <li><span>08</span><strong>Epsilon or strict equality?</strong><em>recovered</em><p>Why floating point forces a choice most tests never make explicit.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="academy-syllabus__group">
          <p class="academy-syllabus__label">03 · Systems &amp; networks</p>
          <ol class="academy-index">
            <li><span>09</span><strong>Cascade models and network diffusion</strong><em>recovered</em><p>How a local shock stays bounded, or becomes a cascade, in a connected field.</p></li>
            <li><span>10</span><strong>Directed and bidirectional flow</strong><em>recovered</em><p>Two very different network behaviours that look identical drawn on paper.</p></li>
            <li><span>11</span><strong>Explicit interfaces over hidden state</strong><em>recovered</em><p>A small design habit that turns silent bugs into loud, early ones.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="academy-syllabus__group">
          <p class="academy-syllabus__label">04 · Practice</p>
          <ol class="academy-index">
            <li><span>12</span><strong>Writing implementation briefs</strong><em>retained</em><p>Scoping a change before writing it, so review has something real to check against.</p></li>
            <li><span>13</span><strong>Reading line reviews</strong><em>retained</em><p>What a careful review is actually looking for, line by line.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="academy-principle shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">The only rule</p>
    <blockquote>
      If the central idea cannot be explained clearly, we probably do not understand it yet.
    </blockquote>
    <p>Simple first. Arbitrarily deep afterwards.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
